---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import RadarWalkthrough from '../../components/RadarWalkthrough.astro';
---
<SiteLayout title="Klimate — Kraig Spear" description="A development preview of Klimate, Kraig Spear’s weather app for planning time outside.">
  <section class="hero wrap" aria-labelledby="klimate-title">
    <a class="text-link" href="/#work">← Selected work</a>
    <p class="eyebrow">Personal project <span class="status">In development</span></p>
    <h1 id="klimate-title">Klimate</h1>
    <div class="hero-bottom"><p>Weather, with a reason to get outside. A native iOS app that brings the forecast, your Activities, and weather radar into one place.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="app-gallery wrap" aria-labelledby="gallery-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2 id="gallery-title">A look inside Klimate</h2><span>Development preview</span></div>
    <div class="app-screens">
      <figure>
        <img src="/images/klimate-weather.webp" width="720" height="1565" alt="Klimate weather screen showing Caledonia’s current conditions and hourly and daily forecasts." fetchpriority="high" />
        <figcaption><strong>Weather</strong>Current conditions and the forecast ahead.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="/images/klimate-activities.webp" width="720" height="1565" alt="Klimate Activities list with an enabled Morning Run activity." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption><strong>Activities</strong>Weather criteria for the things you enjoy.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <video controls playsinline preload="none" poster="/images/klimate-radar-poster.jpg" width="720" height="1566" aria-label="Klimate Radar demonstration" aria-describedby="radar-caption">
          <source src="/videos/klimate-radar.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          Your browser does not support embedded video. <a href="/videos/klimate-radar.mp4">Download the Radar demonstration</a>.
        </video>
        <figcaption id="radar-caption"><strong>Radar</strong>Precipitation animates over Michigan as the timeline advances and the map controls expand. Twelve-second simulator recording, no audio.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="gallery-note">Screens from the app in development. The interface may change before release.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="about wrap" aria-labelledby="activity-story-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">From words to weather criteria</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="activity-story-title">Your plans, expressed in your words.</h2>
      <p>An Activity starts with what you want to do and the conditions that suit it. Apple’s Foundation Models draft weather criteria from your description. You can review and edit those criteria.</p>
      <p>Swift code then evaluates the criteria and optional schedule against forecast data to produce Recommendations: suggested time windows and explanations of the match. This evaluation is deterministic. The model does not generate the forecast.</p>
      <p>I’m building Klimate across the interface and the underlying systems, with particular attention to the handoff between model output, user control, and predictable app behavior.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="engineering wrap" aria-labelledby="engineering-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2 id="engineering-title">Engineering notes</h2><span>Behind the interface</span></div>
    <div class="highlights">
      <article aria-labelledby="activities-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Walkthrough forthcoming</p>
        <h3 id="activities-title">Activities</h3>
        <p>A closer look at moving from a written description to editable criteria, then evaluating those criteria against the forecast.</p>
        <p class="forthcoming-note">The detailed explanation and recorded walkthrough are being prepared.</p>
      </article>
      <article aria-labelledby="radar-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Inside the implementation</p>
        <h3 id="radar-title">Radar</h3>
        <p>How decoded images, frame retention, and a shared memory budget keep radar readable as the timeline advances.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="#radar-engineering">Read the Radar walkthrough <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <RadarWalkthrough />
  <div class="project-end wrap"><a class="text-link" href="/#work">← Back to selected work</a><a class="text-link" href="mailto:kraigspear@gmail.com">Get in touch <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
</SiteLayout>
